content.text:
Confirmed, and it's worse than a style problem — **the code doesn't do what its own comment claims.** The comment says "a human already said yes to this exact proposal," but `if (onEscalate === "approved") continue` waves through *any* escalate, including one the human never saw. If the rule changed between propose and apply, or the row moved, a brand-new escalation gets silently auto-approved. That's a hole I shipped, and it's the real motivation for the idea.

## The two failure modes

**Forgetting.** `onEscalate` is an optional string with a safe-sounding default. Miss it and you get an unclearable card — silently. That's what happened four times.

**Over-granting.** Pass it and you approve *every* escalation on that row, not the one on the card.

A brand fixes the second properly and makes the first loud. Here's the shape:

## The design

Today the "a human approved this" fact is a string literal anyone can type. Make it a value only the approval path can construct:

```ts
declare const approvalBrand: unique symbol;

/** Proof a human approved a SPECIFIC proposal, carrying what they saw. */
export type ApprovalGrant = {
  readonly [approvalBrand]: true;
  readonly proposalRunId: number;
  readonly approverUserId: string;
  /** The fields the rule escalated when the card was minted. */
  readonly grantedFields: readonly string[];
};

/** The only constructor — reads the approved proposal row and proves it. */
export async function grantFromApprovedProposal(params: {
  tx: DbClient;
  proposalRunId: number;
  approverUserId: string | null;
}): Promise<ApprovalGrant>;
```

Then the validators take `grant?: ApprovalGrant` instead of `onEscalate`, and the check becomes scoped rather than blanket:

```ts
if (verdict.outcome === "escalate") {
  const covered = verdict.fields.every((f) => grant?.grantedFields.includes(f));
  if (covered) continue;          // exactly what the human saw
  throw new EntityRowValidationError(...);  // a NEW escalation needs its own card
}
```

That requires recording the verdict's `fields` on the proposal when the card is minted — which the deferral already computes, it just doesn't persist it.

## What this buys

An approval stops being a mode and becomes **evidence**: it names a proposal, an approver, and the exact fields reviewed. Approving "amount over 50k" no longer silently blesses a vendor change the rule started objecting to five minutes later. And you can't fabricate one — there's no public constructor, same as `ValidatedEntityRowPatch`.

## The honest limit

This does **not** make forgetting impossible the way the write brand does. The write brand works because `insertEntityRow` *requires* a branded value — there's no default, so omitting it is a compile error. Here the validator must stay callable without a grant, since ordinary writes have no approval. So a careless apply path could still call the plain validator and get defer semantics — which is precisely the bug the reviewer found in the attribute branch.

Closing that needs a second, cheaper mechanism: make `grant` a **required** parameter of the apply-path functions so it's in scope, and add a scoped `no-restricted-imports` rule so the approval module can't reach the ungranted validator at all. Structural for the correctness half, mechanical for the forgetting half. I'd rather say that plainly than oversell it as airtight.

## Scope

This is a design sketch, not built. It changes the validator signature and adds a persisted field to proposals, so under the repo's rules it needs your sign-off before anyone writes it — this conversation is that ask, not a green light I'm assuming.

I'd also keep it out of #2842. That PR is already ~2,500 lines and has grown three times; this is a clean separate concern. The blanket-approval hole is real but narrow — it needs a rule to change or a row to move between propose and apply — so it doesn't block the current merge.

**Next:** (1) say whether the grant design is worth a PR; (2) meanwhile #2842 still needs the gate + review re-run on the new HEAD, both repeats now. Want me to kick those off?

